# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored and must stay that way — it is
# the only file here that holds a secret.
#
# There are no Bluesky app passwords in this list, deliberately. Users log in
# with atproto OAuth and their tokens live in the sidecar's store, so this
# server never holds a credential belonging to a person.

# --- required ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The model provider. Every turn anyone takes bills to this one key, which is
# what the per-user rate limits below are for.
GREENPT_API_KEY=

# --- for the keyword-index crawler (index/crawl.py) -------------------------

# The crawler's own search account, as a scoped app password. The production
# product bans app passwords for its users on purpose; this is an experiment —
# one account, read-only search, revocable in Bluesky -> App Passwords. Put
# the *generated* password here, not your main account password.
# INDEX_BLUESKY_HANDLE=
# INDEX_BLUESKY_APP_PASSWORD=

# The exploration loop's tuning. The window is applied on each post's own
# createdAt (the public search API has no reliable date range).
# INDEX_PASS_RATE=0.5
# INDEX_POSTS=10
# INDEX_WINDOW_DAYS=30

# --- generated for you ------------------------------------------------------

# opencode's own server password: the second lock, behind the proxy. start.sh
# generates one and appends it here on first run, so leave it unset the first
# time. The username is literally `opencode`.
# OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD=

# --- required once it is reachable from outside -----------------------------

# The public origin, e.g. https://aligned.click. Setting it does three things:
# it starts the cloudflared tunnel, it makes the session cookie Secure, and it
# is the origin the OAuth client metadata is published under. Leave it empty to
# run on localhost only.
PUBLIC_URL=

# --- optional ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Where published conversations are read. A separate host on purpose, so a
# post that links back stays readable when this machine is off.
# READER_URL=https://read.aligned.click

# An ntfy topic URL — https://ntfy.sh/<topic>, or your own ntfy. A waitlist
# nobody is told about is a file that fills up quietly. The notification is sent
# ntfy's way: the message is the request body, the title and tags are headers.
# Anything else that accepts a plain-text POST will also work; Discord will not,
# since it wants its own JSON shape.
# WAITLIST_WEBHOOK=
#
# An ntfy access token (`tk_…`), if the topic is reserved to your account. Worth
# setting rather than relying on an unguessable name: on ntfy.sh a bare topic is
# public to anyone who knows it, and this one carries the handle of everybody
# asking to join an invite-only site. A guessed topic is a list of who wants in.
# WAITLIST_TOKEN=

# Turns on opencode's built-in `websearch`, which is otherwise available only
# to the `opencode` provider — and ours is GreenPT, so without this the tool is
# not registered and the model reports having no such tool. It needs no key:
# opencode talks to Exa's hosted MCP unauthenticated (`EXA_API_KEY` is only for
# bringing your own account).
#
# Read by opencode, not by anything Python here. It reaches it because
# aligned-opencode.service loads this file as its EnvironmentFile.
#
# Only `builder` may call it — see the agent block in agent/opencode.json.
# Not because search is dangerous, but because search results are text from
# strangers arriving in a session that may run commands, and because a Bluesky
# reading agent that quietly browses the web is not the thing anyone was
# offered. Widening it to everyone means adding `websearch` to the top-level
# `tools` block, which is the list the proxy hands out.
# OPENCODE_ENABLE_EXA=1

# Per-user limits. Not for abuse — for the loop: an agent that gets stuck is
# the normal way one key becomes expensive.
# RATE_PER_MINUTE=120
# PROMPTS_PER_DAY=300

# Ports. Only the proxy is ever exposed; the rest bind 127.0.0.1.
# PROXY_PORT=8778
# OPENCODE_PORT=4096
# OAUTH_PORT=4098

# --- only to republish a lexicon schema -------------------------------------

# The account the `com.atproto.lexicon.schema` records belong to. A schema is
# published by the authority the NSID names, not by whoever is chatting, so
# this is set in a shell for that one job and never for the server.
# ADMIN_DID=

# --- for running a tool by hand, never for the server -----------------------

# Which account a directly-invoked tool acts as. The server does not set this
# and must not: a tool learns whose call it is from the session that caused it,
# and a server-wide default would make every user's call look like one person's.
# ACTING_DID=
